 Your Protest**\n\n### 1. **Determine the Type of Protest**\nThere are **two main types of protests** you can file with the IRS:\n\n| Type of Protest | Eligibility | Purpose |\n|----------------|-------------|---------|\n| **Form 12153 (Request for Taxpayer Advocate Service Assistance)** | For claims under $50,000 | Used to request help from the **Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS)** |\n| **Written Protest** | For claims **$50,000 or more** | Used to formally challenge an IRS decision |\n\nSince you're asking about the **written protest**, we'll focus on that.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Find the Correct IRS Service Center**\nTo file a **written protest**, you need to send your letter to the **appropriate IRS Service Center**. The correct one depends on the **type of tax issue** you're protesting.\n\n#### **Common IRS Service Centers for Written Protests**\n| Type of Issue | Service Center | Address |\n|---------------|----------------|---------|\n| **Income Tax** | **IRS Correspondence Processing Center (CP-42)** | P.O. Box 29584, Cincinnati, OH 45229 |\n| **Estate or Gift Tax** | **IRS Estate and Gift Tax Service Center (CP-55)** | P.O. Box 51018, Chicago, IL 60651 |\n| **Excise Tax (e.g., alcohol, tobacco, firearms)** | **IRS Excise Tax Service Center (CP-66)** | P.O. Box 34469, Atlanta, GA 30384 |\n| **Employment Taxes (e.g., payroll, unemployment)** | **IRS Employment Tax Service Center (CP-77)** | P.O. Box 1666, Austin, TX 78761 |\n| **Other Taxes (e.g., property, sales, excise)** | **IRS Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS)** | 1200 New York Ave NW, Washington"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Could you explain what the Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS) does and how I can contact them if I need assistance with my protest or other IRS-related issues? I want to understand my options for getting help.", "persuasion_strategy": 0}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Absolutely! The **Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS)** is a valuable resource for taxpayers who are facing **difficulties wi